@cindex client parameter
As explained in @ref{Architecture}, the @emph{middle module} contains
customizable generic functions with a client parameter which implement
the behavior of package system operations.  There is mostly but not
quite a one-to-one correspondence between the generic functions in this
module and the @commonlisp{} package system operators.  One exception
are ``accessor-like'' operators such as @speclink{f,symbol-name} or
@speclink{f,package-used-by-list}.  Those operators are implemented by
generic functions in the @ref{Low Module,low module}.  The other
exceptions are the generic functions concerned with updating packages
and handling variance such as @genref{update-package,@middlepackage{}}.

The functions in this module accept only strings when names are required
and only symbol objects and package objects (not designators) where
symbols and packages are required.

Old:
@quotation
The ``low'' system exists in a single version, common to the extrinsic
and the intrinsic systems.  The ``low'' system contains the bulk of
the functionality of Parcl, but the operators in this system are
defined as generic functions taking an additional @t{client}
parameter compared to the standard operators.  A large part of the
code for the package system consists of checking for symbol conflicts
and other exceptional situations, and those situations are handled by
the signaling of correctable errors, as required by the standard.
All this code is present in the ``low'' system.
@end quotation

@node Middle Symbol Functions
@section Middle Symbol Functions

@defgena{keywordp,@middlepackage{}} client object

Return true if @var{object} is a keyword symbol @emph{in the package
system specified by @var{client}}.

@middleimplements{f,keywordp}

The default method on this generic function returns true if
@code{(@genref{symbolp,@lowpackage{}} @var{client} @var{object})} returns
true and
@code{(@genref{symbol-package,@lowpackage{}} @var{client} @var{object})}
returns the package named @code{"KEYWORD"}.
@end deffn

@node Middle Package Functions
@section Middle Package Functions

Most standard @commonlisp{} operators and the corresponding @ref{High
Module,high-level} functions in @sysname{} that operate package objects,
like @speclink{f,package-name} for example, are like readers.  For
reader-like functions, @sysname{} has the high-level function,
@funref{package-name,@toppackage{}} in the example, directly call the
@ref{Low Module,low-level} function, @genref{name,@lowpackage{}} in the
example.  Clients can customize the low-level function and there is
nothing an intermediate function in the middle module could add.  The
only exception is the following function because @sysname{} does not
handle the information about shadowing as if there was corresponding
slot in the package object.  Instead, the information is computed when
needed as explained below:

@defgena{shadowing-symbols,@middlepackage{}} client package

Return the list of shadowing symbols of @var{package}.

@middleimplements{f,shadowing-symbols}

@cindex shadow status
The default method calls @genref{map-symbol-entries,@lowpackage{}} and
returns symbols the @var{shadow-status} of which is @emph{true}.
@end deffn

The following generic functions do not correspond to standard
@commonlisp{} operators but instead exist to allow clients to customize
the process of creating and updating packages including handling of
package variance.  While clients could use this non-standard protocol
directly to create and update packages, the usual call chain is from the
expansion produced by @speclink{m,defpackage}
@c TODO: @macref{defpackage,@toppackage{}}
to @genref{ensure-package,@middlepackage{}}.

@defgena{ensure-package,@middlepackage{}} @
  client name @pkey{} @allowotherkeys{}

Either create or update the package named @var{name} according to the
options supplied as keyword arguments and return it.

The default method on this generic function calls
@genref{find-package-using-package,@middlepackage{}} and then@comma{}
regardless of whether the result of that call was a package object or
@code{nil}@comma{} applies
@genref{ensure-package-using-package,@middlepackage{}} to
@var{client}@comma{} the result and @var{args}.
@end deffn

@defgena{ensure-package-using-package,@middlepackage{}} @
  client existing-package name @pkey{} @allowotherkeys{}

Apply the options supplied as keyword arguments to either a new package
or the existing package @var{existing-package} and return the new or
updated package.

The caller has to ensure that this function is called in one of the
following two ways.  If a package named @var{name} does not yet
exist@comma{} @var{existing-package} is @code{nil} and this generic
function has to create a new package.  If a package named @var{name}
exists@comma{} the value of @var{existing-package} is that package ad
this generic function has to update @var{existing-package}.

The default methods on this generic function handle those two cases: one
method is specialized to @var{existing-package} being @code{null} and
calls @genref{make-package,@middlepackage{}} followed by
@genref{update-package,@middlepackage{}}.  The other default method
handles the case of @var{existing-package} being an existing package.
The method computes differences between the existing package and the
desired new definition of the package as specified by the keyword
arguments the method receives.  For each incompatible change@comma{} the
method calls @genref{note-variance,@middlepackage{}} to either abort the
whole operation or select an action that resolves the incompatibility.
If the operation is not aborted@comma{} this method calls
@genref{update-package,@middlepackage{}} with the list of actions that
realize the new definition and resolve all incompatibilities if any.
@end deffn

@defgena{note-variance,@middlepackage{}} client package aspect event value

Indicate that an aspect of the new definition of @var{package} is at
variance with the current state of @var{package}.

@var{aspect} is the aspect of @var{package} that is at variance with the
current state @var{package}.  Possible values @code{:use}@comma{}
@code{:shadow} and @code{:export} as well potential additions by
clients.

@var{event} is either @code{:add} or @code{:remove}

@var{value} is either a package object or a symbol object.  @var{value}
is the object the addition to/removal from @var{aspect} of @var{package}
causes the variance issue being reported.
@end deffn

@defgena{update-package,@middlepackage{}} @
  client package @pkey{} @allowotherkeys{}

Modify @var{package} according to the supplied keyword arguments and
return the modified @var{package}.

Depending on the value of @var{client}@comma{} different modifications and thus
keyword arguments may be accepted@comma{} but at least the following keyword
arguments are always recognized:

@table @code

@item :nicknames @var{names}

@item :shadow @var{symbols}

@item :shadowing-import @var{symbols}

@item :use @var{packages}

@item :unuse @var{packages}

@item :import @var{symbol-names}

@item :intern @var{symbols}

Intern @var{symbols} into @var{package}

@item :unintern @var{symbols}

Intern @var{symbols} from @var{package}

@item :export @var{symbols}

Export @var{symbols} from @var{package}

@item :unexport @var{symbols}

Unexport @var{symbols} from @var{package}

@item :documentation @var{new-value}

Set the documentation of @var{package} to @var{new-value} which is
either @code{nil} or a string.

@end table

The changes are performed in the order given in the specification of
@speclink{f,defpackage} not in the order in which the keyword arguments
are supplied.  TODO mention order for removal changes
@end deffn

